const EXTRA_LESSONS = [["HTML Semantic Elements","HTML","Beginner","🏛️","blue","Use semantic tags to give page sections clear meaning.","Semantic HTML uses elements such as header, nav, main, section, article, aside, and footer. Meaningful structure helps accessibility and maintenance.","<main><article><h1>Study Plan</h1><p>Learn every day.</p></article></main>","Replace generic div containers in a small page with semantic elements.","Why use semantic HTML?","It gives content clearer meaning and improves accessibility."],["HTML Tables","HTML","Beginner","📊","blue","Display structured rows and columns with accessible tables.","Tables are for tabular data. Use table, thead, tbody, tr, th, and td. Headers should describe their columns.","<table><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Score</th></tr><tr><td>HTML</td><td>90</td></tr></table>","Build a marks table with a header row and four student rows.","Which tag represents a table header cell?","<th>"],["HTML Audio and Video","HTML","Beginner","🎬","blue","Embed media directly into a webpage.","The audio and video elements can provide controls. Add useful fallback text and consider captions for video.","<video controls width=\"480\"><source src=\"lesson.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"></video>","Add a short tutorial video with controls and a descriptive caption.","Which attribute displays playback controls?","controls"],["HTML Meta and SEO Basics","HTML","Novice","🔍","blue","Understand page metadata and basic search-friendly structure.","The title and description metadata help browsers and search systems understand a page. Use one clear title and useful headings.","<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Learn web development with StudyHub.\">","Add a meaningful title and description to a practice page.","Which meta name is commonly used for a page description?","description"],["HTML Data Attributes","HTML","Novice","🏷️","blue","Store custom data on elements for JavaScript.","data-* attributes let you attach small custom values to HTML without inventing nonstandard attributes. JavaScript can read them through dataset.","<button data-topic=\"python\">Python</button>\n<script>console.log(document.querySelector('button').dataset.topic)</script>","Add data-topic values to three lesson buttons and read them with JavaScript.","What prefix starts a custom data attribute?","data-"],["HTML Dialogs","HTML","Intermediate","💬","blue","Create native dialog boxes for focused interactions.","The dialog element can represent a modal or non-modal conversation. JavaScript can call showModal() and close().","<dialog id=\"info\">Welcome!</dialog>\n<script>info.showModal()</script>","Create a dialog that explains a lesson before the user starts it.","Which method opens a dialog as a modal?","showModal()"],["HTML Details and Summary","HTML","Beginner","📦","blue","Build expandable information without complex JavaScript.","details creates a disclosure widget and summary provides its visible label. It is useful for FAQs and optional explanations.","<details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>CSS styles pages.</p></details>","Create five expandable FAQ items.","Which element is the clickable label inside details?","summary"],["HTML Validation Attributes","HTML","Novice","✅","blue","Use built-in browser validation to improve forms.","Attributes such as required, minlength, maxlength, min, max, pattern, and type help browsers validate input before submission.","<input type=\"email\" required minlength=\"6\">","Add validation rules to a registration form.","Which attribute makes a field mandatory?","required"],["HTML Canvas Basics","HTML","Advanced","🖌️","blue","Draw simple graphics with the canvas API.","canvas provides a drawing surface. JavaScript gets a 2D context and can draw shapes, text, and images.","<canvas id=\"c\" width=\"300\" height=\"150\"></canvas>\n<script>const ctx=c.getContext('2d');ctx.fillRect(20,20,80,50);</script>","Draw a simple bar chart with three rectangles.","Which method gets the 2D drawing context?","getContext('2d')"],["HTML Accessibility Landmarks","HTML","Advanced","♿","blue","Make navigation and page regions easier for assistive technology.","Landmark elements such as nav, main, aside, and footer help users move around a page. Prefer native semantic elements before adding ARIA.","<nav aria-label=\"Main navigation\">...</nav>","Review a page and identify its navigation, main content, and complementary regions.","What should you prefer before adding ARIA roles?","Native semantic HTML"],["CSS Box Model","CSS","Beginner","📦","purple","Understand content, padding, border, and margin.","Every element is laid out as a box. The box model explains how content, padding, border, and margin affect size and spacing.",".card{width:300px;padding:20px;border:1px solid #ccc;margin:16px;}","Inspect a card and calculate its total outer size.","What sits between content and border?","Padding"],["CSS Selectors","CSS","Beginner","🎯","purple","Target elements precisely with common selectors.","Type, class, ID, attribute, descendant, child, and pseudo-class selectors let you style different groups of elements.",".card > h2 { margin:0; }\ninput[type=\"email\"] { border:1px solid; }","Write selectors for cards, buttons, and email inputs.","Which selector targets an element with a class?",".className"],["CSS Specificity","CSS","Intermediate","⚖️","purple","Understand why one CSS rule overrides another.","Specificity determines which competing selector wins. IDs are stronger than classes, and classes are stronger than element selectors. Source order matters when specificity is equal.","button {color:black}.primary {color:gold}","Fix a button whose intended class style is being overridden.","Which is more specific: .button or #save?","#save"],["CSS Positioning","CSS","Intermediate","📍","purple","Place elements using static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky positioning.","Positioning changes how an element participates in layout. Relative creates a positioning context, absolute positions against a containing block, fixed follows the viewport, and sticky can pin during scrolling.",".badge{position:absolute;top:8px;right:8px;}","Place a notification badge in the corner of a card.","Which positioning mode can stay attached to the viewport?","fixed"],["CSS Z-Index","CSS","Intermediate","🪜","purple","Control which positioned elements appear above others.","z-index controls stacking order in applicable stacking contexts. Large numbers are not a substitute for understanding the containing stacking context.",".modal{position:fixed;z-index:1000;}","Create a modal that appears above the page header.","What property controls stacking order?","z-index"],["CSS Gradients","CSS","Beginner","🌈","purple","Create smooth color transitions without image files.","Linear and radial gradients can be used as backgrounds. They are useful for subtle hero backgrounds, cards, and highlights.","background:linear-gradient(135deg,#111,#8b6f3d);","Create a subtle two-color hero gradient.","Which CSS function creates a linear gradient?","linear-gradient()"],["CSS Shadows","CSS","Beginner","🌑","purple","Add depth with box-shadow and text-shadow.","Shadows should support hierarchy rather than overwhelm the interface. Control horizontal offset, vertical offset, blur, spread, and color.","box-shadow:0 12px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.15);","Add a soft shadow to a study card.","Which property adds a box shadow?","box-shadow"],["CSS Typography","CSS","Intermediate","🔤","purple","Improve readability with font family, size, weight, line height, and spacing.","Typography affects hierarchy and comfort. Use readable sizes, adequate line-height, and a clear scale between headings and body text.","body{font-size:16px;line-height:1.6} h1{font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem)}","Create a heading and paragraph with a clear type hierarchy.","Which property controls vertical space between lines?","line-height"],["CSS Clamp and Fluid Sizing","CSS","Advanced","📏","purple","Make dimensions adapt smoothly between screen sizes.","clamp() accepts a minimum, preferred, and maximum value. It is useful for responsive typography and spacing without many breakpoints.","h1{font-size:clamp(2rem,6vw,5rem);}","Make a hero heading scale between phone and desktop.","What function combines minimum, preferred, and maximum values?","clamp()"],["CSS Container Queries","CSS","Expert","🧩","purple","Style components based on their container rather than the viewport.","Container queries let reusable components adapt to the space they occupy. Mark a container with container-type and query it with @container.",".card-wrap{container-type:inline-size}@container(max-width:500px){.card{grid-template-columns:1fr}}","Build a card component that changes layout when its parent becomes narrow.","Which at-rule defines a container query?","@container"],["CSS Logical Properties","CSS","Advanced","↔️","purple","Write layouts that adapt better to writing direction.","Logical properties such as margin-inline, padding-block, and inset-inline-start describe relationships instead of physical left and right sides.",".card{padding-inline:20px;margin-block:16px;}","Replace physical horizontal spacing with logical properties.","What does padding-inline control?","Inline-start and inline-end padding"],["JavaScript let const","JavaScript","Beginner","📦","orange","Choose appropriate variable declarations in modern JavaScript.","Use const when a binding should not be reassigned and let when it needs reassignment. Avoid var in new code unless you specifically need its older behavior.","const name='AJ';\nlet score=0;\nscore+=10;","Rewrite a small script using const and let appropriately.","Which declaration is best when a binding will not be reassigned?","const"],["JavaScript Data Types","JavaScript","Beginner","🧱","orange","Recognize strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, objects, and arrays.","JavaScript values have different types. Understanding types helps prevent accidental comparisons and operations.","const age=15;\nconst active=true;\nconst name='AJ';","Create variables representing a student's name, age, score, and active status.","Which type represents true or false?","boolean"],["JavaScript Template Literals","JavaScript","Beginner","📝","orange","Build readable strings with embedded expressions.","Template literals use backticks and ${} expressions. They are convenient for messages and HTML snippets.","const name='AJ';\nconst text=`Hello ${name}!`;","Create a progress message showing a user's completed lesson count.","Which syntax inserts an expression into a template literal?","${expression}"],["JavaScript Destructuring","JavaScript","Intermediate","📦","orange","Extract values from arrays and object properties cleanly.","Destructuring assigns parts of an array or object to variables in one statement.","const user={name:'AJ',level:'Expert'};\nconst {name,level}=user;","Extract name and score from a student object.","What feature extracts object properties into variables?","Destructuring"],["JavaScript Spread and Rest","JavaScript","Intermediate","✨","orange","Copy, combine, and collect values with ... syntax.","Spread expands iterable or object values. Rest collects remaining values. Both can make data transformations concise.","const updated={...user,score:95};\nfunction sum(...nums){return nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)}","Create a new object without mutating the original student record.","Which syntax copies properties into a new object?","Object spread (...)"],["JavaScript Array Map","JavaScript","Intermediate","🗺️","orange","Transform every item in an array with map().","map creates a new array by applying a function to each item. It does not change the original array by itself.","const scores=[70,80,90];\nconst boosted=scores.map(s=>s+5);","Convert a list of lesson titles into uppercase titles.","What does map() return?","A new array of transformed values."],["JavaScript Array Reduce","JavaScript","Advanced","➕","orange","Combine array values into one result.","reduce processes an array while carrying an accumulator. It can calculate totals, build objects, or group information.","const total=[20,30,50].reduce((sum,n)=>sum+n,0);","Calculate the total XP from completed lessons.","What is the accumulator used for?","It carries the result between reduce iterations."],["JavaScript Optional Chaining","JavaScript","Intermediate","🛡️","orange","Safely read nested properties when intermediate values may be missing.","Optional chaining uses ?. to stop a property access chain when a value is null or undefined.","const city=user?.profile?.city;","Read a user's optional profile name without throwing an error.","Which operator safely accesses an optional property?","?."],["JavaScript Nullish Coalescing","JavaScript","Intermediate","🔧","orange","Provide a fallback only when a value is null or undefined.","The ?? operator differs from || because valid falsy values such as 0 and an empty string are not replaced.","const xp=user.xp ?? 0;","Give a missing lesson count a default of zero.","Which operator checks for nullish values?","??"],["JavaScript Classes","JavaScript","Advanced","🏗️","orange","Model related data and behavior with classes.","Classes define constructors and methods for creating objects with shared behavior. Use them when they make the model clearer, not simply because they exist.","class Student{constructor(name){this.name=name} greet(){return `Hi ${this.name}`}}","Create a Student class with name and completed count.","Which method initializes a new class instance?","constructor"],["JavaScript Modules","JavaScript","Advanced","🧩","orange","Split JavaScript into maintainable files with import and export.","ES modules let files export values and import them elsewhere. Modules reduce global variables and make larger projects easier to organize.","export function add(a,b){return a+b}\nimport {add} from './math.js';","Move a utility function into a separate module.","Which keywords share values between modules?","export and import"],["JavaScript Event Delegation","JavaScript","Expert","🖱️","orange","Handle many dynamic elements with one event listener.","Event delegation listens on a common ancestor and checks event.target or closest(). It is useful for lists where items may be added later.","list.addEventListener('click',e=>{const btn=e.target.closest('button');if(btn)console.log(btn.dataset.id)});","Build a lesson list where one parent handles all button clicks.","Why can event delegation help dynamic lists?","One parent listener can handle current and future child elements."],["JavaScript Debounce","JavaScript","Expert","⏱️","orange","Delay frequent work such as search requests until typing pauses.","Debouncing groups rapid events and runs a function after a quiet period. It can reduce expensive search or network operations.","let timer;\ninput.addEventListener('input',()=>{clearTimeout(timer);timer=setTimeout(search,300)});","Debounce a lesson search input by 300 milliseconds.","What problem does debouncing reduce?","Repeated work caused by rapid events."],["Python Strings","Python","Beginner","🔤","green","Manipulate text with indexing, slicing, and string methods.","Python strings are sequences. Methods such as lower, upper, strip, replace, split, and join are useful for cleaning and transforming text.","text=' Study Python '\nprint(text.strip().lower())","Clean a username by removing surrounding spaces and converting it to lowercase.","Which method removes surrounding whitespace?","strip()"],["Python Lists","Python","Beginner","📋","green","Create, update, and iterate over ordered collections.","Lists are mutable ordered collections. Use append, extend, insert, remove, pop, and slicing to work with them.","topics=['HTML','CSS'];topics.append('JS')","Build a five-item study plan and add a sixth topic.","Which method adds one item to the end?","append()"],["Python Tuples and Sets","Python","Novice","🔢","green","Use tuples for fixed groups and sets for unique values.","Tuples are immutable sequences. Sets contain unique elements and support operations such as union and intersection.","skills={'HTML','CSS','HTML'}\nprint(skills)","Remove duplicate subject names using a set.","Which collection automatically keeps only unique values?","set"],["Python List Comprehensions","Python","Intermediate","⚡","green","Build lists concisely from existing iterables.","List comprehensions combine iteration and optional conditions into a readable expression.","squares=[n*n for n in range(1,6)]","Create a list containing only even numbers from 1 through 20.","What does a list comprehension create?","A new list from an iterable expression."],["Python Exceptions","Python","Intermediate","🚨","green","Handle expected runtime errors with try and except.","Exceptions let programs respond to failures instead of crashing unexpectedly. Catch specific errors when possible and keep the protected block small.","try:\n    value=int(input('Number: '))\nexcept ValueError:\n    print('Enter a valid number')","Handle invalid numeric input safely.","Which block handles a caught exception?","except"],["Python Modules and Imports","Python","Intermediate","📦","green","Reuse code from modules and packages.","A Python file can act as a module. import loads a module, while from module import name imports selected values.","import math\nprint(math.sqrt(81))","Import a standard-library module and use one of its functions.","Which keyword loads a module?","import"],["Python Classes","Python","Advanced","🏗️","green","Create objects with shared data and behavior.","Classes define object structure and methods. __init__ initializes instance attributes and self refers to the current instance.","class Student:\n    def __init__(self,name):\n        self.name=name","Create a Student class with name and score properties.","What does self refer to?","The current instance."],["Python Lambda and Sorting","Python","Advanced","↕️","green","Sort data using key functions and concise lambdas.","sorted() returns a new sorted list. The key argument controls what value is compared. Lambdas are useful for short key functions.","students=[{'name':'A','score':80},{'name':'B','score':95}]\nstudents=sorted(students,key=lambda s:s['score'],reverse=True)","Sort students by score from highest to lowest.","Which argument controls the sort key?","key"],["Python JSON","Python","Advanced","🧾","green","Convert between Python data and JSON.","The json module serializes Python dictionaries and lists to JSON and parses JSON back into Python values.","import json\ntext=json.dumps({'xp':100})\ndata=json.loads(text)","Save a small progress dictionary as JSON text.","Which function converts a Python object to JSON text?","json.dumps()"],["Python Virtual Environments","Python","Expert","🧪","green","Isolate project dependencies with virtual environments.","Virtual environments keep one project's packages separate from another. Create one with python -m venv and activate it before installing dependencies.","python -m venv .venv\npython -m pip install requests","Create a virtual environment for a practice project and install one package.","Why use a virtual environment?","To isolate project dependencies."],["Python Testing with unittest","Python","Expert","🧪","green","Write repeatable checks for Python functions.","unittest provides test cases, assertions, setup methods, and a test runner. Tests should verify expected behavior and important edge cases.","import unittest\nclass TestAdd(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add(self): self.assertEqual(2+3,5)","Write three tests for a small calculator function.","Which assertion checks equality?","assertEqual()"],["SQL WHERE and ORDER BY","SQL","Beginner","🔎","purple","Filter rows and control result order.","WHERE selects rows matching a condition. ORDER BY sorts the returned rows ascending or descending.","SELECT * FROM students WHERE score >= 80 ORDER BY score DESC;","Find students above 70 and order them by score.","Which keyword sorts query results?","ORDER BY"],["SQL GROUP BY","SQL","Intermediate","📊","purple","Group rows so aggregate functions can summarize them.","GROUP BY creates groups for aggregate functions such as COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX.","SELECT subject, COUNT(*) FROM lessons GROUP BY subject;","Count how many records belong to each subject.","Which clause creates groups for aggregates?","GROUP BY"],["SQL HAVING","SQL","Intermediate","🎯","purple","Filter grouped results after aggregation.","WHERE filters individual rows before grouping. HAVING filters groups after aggregate calculations.","SELECT subject, COUNT(*) AS total FROM lessons GROUP BY subject HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;","Show only subjects with more than five lessons.","Which clause filters grouped results?","HAVING"],["SQL Primary Keys","SQL","Beginner","🔑","purple","Identify rows uniquely with primary keys.","A primary key uniquely identifies each row. It should be stable and not duplicated. Many databases use integer or UUID identifiers.","CREATE TABLE students(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,name TEXT);","Design a table with a primary key and two data columns.","What is the main purpose of a primary key?","Uniquely identify each row."],["SQL Foreign Keys","SQL","Intermediate","🔗","purple","Represent relationships between tables.","A foreign key references a key in another table. It helps enforce valid relationships between related records.","FOREIGN KEY(student_id) REFERENCES students(id)","Connect enrollments to students with a foreign key.","What does a foreign key reference?","A key in another table."],["SQL Transactions","SQL","Advanced","🔒","purple","Group related database changes into an all-or-nothing operation.","Transactions help maintain consistency when several changes must succeed together. Typical operations are BEGIN, COMMIT, and ROLLBACK.","BEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance=balance-100 WHERE id=1;\nCOMMIT;","Describe when a transaction is useful for a multi-step update.","What does ROLLBACK do?","It undoes uncommitted transaction changes."],["SQL Indexes","SQL","Advanced","⚡","purple","Speed up suitable queries with indexes.","Indexes provide additional lookup structures. They can make reads faster but consume storage and can add write overhead, so index columns used by important queries.","CREATE INDEX idx_students_score ON students(score);","Choose one frequently searched column and explain why it may need an index.","What is the main purpose of an index?","Speed up suitable data lookups."],["SQL Views","SQL","Expert","👓","purple","Create reusable named queries with database views.","A view stores a query definition that can simplify repeated reporting queries and provide a controlled interface to data.","CREATE VIEW top_students AS SELECT name,score FROM students WHERE score>=90;","Create a view that shows only active students.","What does a view represent?","A reusable query exposed like a table."],["Git Status and Diff","Git","Beginner","🔍","dark","Inspect working-tree changes before committing.","git status shows changed and untracked files. git diff shows line-level changes. Reviewing both helps prevent accidental commits.","git status\ngit diff","Modify a file and inspect the exact diff before staging it.","Which command shows line-level unstaged changes?","git diff"],["Git Commit Messages","Git","Beginner","📝","dark","Write useful commits that explain focused changes.","A good commit is small and describes what changed. Clear history makes debugging, review, and rollback easier.","git commit -m \"Add lesson search\"","Create three focused commits with descriptive messages.","What makes a commit message useful?","It clearly describes the focused change."],["Git Restore and Reset Basics","Git","Intermediate","↩️","dark","Understand common ways to undo local changes.","git restore can discard working-tree changes or restore files from another state. Reset can move HEAD and should be used carefully, especially with shared history.","git restore styles.css","Make a harmless local change and practice restoring it.","Which command can restore a working-tree file?","git restore"],["Git Merge Conflicts","Git","Intermediate","⚔️","dark","Resolve conflicts when two changes affect the same lines.","A merge conflict means Git cannot automatically combine changes. Inspect conflict markers, choose the intended content, remove markers, test, then stage and commit.","<<<<<<< HEAD\ncurrent\n=======\nincoming\n>>>>>>> feature","Create a tiny conflict in a practice repository and resolve it.","What should you do after resolving conflict markers?","Test the result, stage the file, and complete the merge."],["Git Tags and Releases","Git","Advanced","🏷️","dark","Mark important repository versions with tags.","Tags give stable names to commits, often for releases. A release can attach notes and downloadable assets to a tag.","git tag v1.0.0\ngit push origin v1.0.0","Tag a finished practice project as version 1.0.0.","What is a tag useful for?","Giving an important commit a stable version name."],["Git Ignore Files","Git","Beginner","🙈","dark","Keep generated and private files out of version control.","A .gitignore file tells Git which paths to ignore. Common examples include dependency folders, build output, logs, and local environment files.","node_modules/\n.env\n*.log","Create a .gitignore for a small web project.","Which file defines ignored paths?",".gitignore"],["HTTP Methods","Web","Beginner","🌐","blue","Understand common HTTP request methods.","GET commonly retrieves data, POST sends data to create or trigger work, PUT replaces a resource, PATCH partially updates it, and DELETE removes it. Exact API semantics should be documented.","GET /api/lessons\nPOST /api/view","Map five example actions to suitable HTTP methods.","Which method is commonly used to retrieve a resource?","GET"],["HTTP Status Codes","Web","Beginner","🚦","blue","Interpret common response status codes.","2xx indicates success, 3xx redirects, 4xx client-side request problems, and 5xx server-side failures. Common examples include 200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 404, and 500.","200 OK\n404 Not Found\n500 Internal Server Error","Create a reference card for ten common status codes.","What does 404 usually mean?","The requested resource was not found."],["Fetch Error Handling","Web","Intermediate","🛡️","blue","Handle failed network requests and bad responses clearly.","fetch rejects for some network failures but does not automatically reject for HTTP 404 or 500. Check response.ok and use try/catch around asynchronous work.","try{const r=await fetch('/api');if(!r.ok)throw new Error(r.status)}catch(e){console.error(e)}","Add loading, success, and error states to an API request.","Does fetch automatically throw for HTTP 404?","No. Check response.ok or status."],["JSON Structures","Web","Beginner","🧾","blue","Read nested objects and arrays in JSON responses.","JSON supports objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null. Access nested values carefully and validate assumptions about response shape.","{\"user\":{\"name\":\"AJ\"},\"scores\":[90,85]}","Read a nested name and calculate an average from a JSON response.","What are two common JSON container types?","Objects and arrays."],["REST API Design","Web","Advanced","🧭","blue","Design predictable resource-oriented API endpoints.","REST-style APIs often use nouns for resources, HTTP methods for actions, status codes for outcomes, and consistent JSON shapes. Good APIs document validation and errors.","GET /api/students\nGET /api/students/42\nPOST /api/students","Design endpoints for lessons and progress.","What should resource paths generally represent?","Resources rather than UI actions."],["CORS Basics","Web","Advanced","🌍","blue","Understand browser cross-origin rules and server responses.","Browsers restrict some cross-origin requests. Servers can opt into allowed origins and methods using CORS response headers. CORS is a browser policy, not an authentication system.","Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com","Explain why a frontend on one origin may need CORS permission from an API.","What does CORS control?","Which cross-origin browser requests are permitted."],["Browser Cookies","Web","Advanced","🍪","blue","Understand how cookies store small pieces of browser-associated data.","Cookies can be sent automatically with requests. Security-related flags include Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite. Authentication cookies require careful server-side design.","Set-Cookie: session=abc; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax","List three security attributes useful for session cookies.","Which flag prevents JavaScript from reading a cookie?","HttpOnly"],["Web Accessibility Keyboard Navigation","Accessibility","Intermediate","⌨️","pink","Make controls usable without a mouse.","Keyboard users need logical focus order, visible focus indicators, and controls that can be reached and activated with standard keys. Prefer native buttons and links.","button:focus-visible{outline:3px solid currentColor;}","Navigate your page using only Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter, and Space.","Why should focus indicators remain visible?","They show keyboard users where they are on the page."],["Color Contrast","Accessibility","Beginner","◐","pink","Choose text and interface colors that remain readable.","Sufficient contrast helps users read content, especially in bright or low-vision conditions. Check text, icons, borders, and focus indicators instead of relying on color alone.","body{color:#222;background:#fff;}","Audit three text combinations and improve any weak contrast.","Why is contrast important?","It improves readability and accessibility."],["Reduced Motion","Accessibility","Advanced","🫧","pink","Respect users who prefer less animation.","The prefers-reduced-motion media feature lets you reduce nonessential motion. Keep essential state changes understandable without animation.","@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{scroll-behavior:auto!important;animation:none!important;transition:none!important}}","Add a reduced-motion rule to an animated page.","Which media feature detects a reduced-motion preference?","prefers-reduced-motion"],["ARIA Basics","Accessibility","Advanced","🏷️","pink","Use ARIA carefully when native HTML is not enough.","ARIA adds accessibility semantics, but incorrect ARIA can make interfaces worse. Use native elements first and add roles or states only when necessary.","<button aria-expanded=\"false\">Lessons</button>","Inspect a custom dropdown and identify which state it needs to expose.","What should be preferred over custom ARIA widgets?","Native semantic HTML controls when available."],["Responsive Images","Performance","Intermediate","🖼️","blue","Deliver appropriate image sizes for different screens.","Responsive images can use srcset and sizes so browsers choose a suitable resource. This can reduce bandwidth while keeping images sharp.","<img src=\"small.jpg\" srcset=\"small.jpg 480w, large.jpg 1200w\" sizes=\"100vw\" alt=\"\">","Add srcset to a hero image with two sizes.","Which attribute lists alternative image resources?","srcset"],["Lazy Loading","Performance","Beginner","🐢","blue","Delay noncritical images until they are near the viewport.","Native loading=\"lazy\" can defer offscreen images. Lazy loading should not be used blindly for important above-the-fold content.","<img src=\"lesson.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Lesson\">","Add lazy loading to a long lesson gallery.","Which attribute enables native image lazy loading?","loading=\"lazy\""],["Browser DevTools","Debugging","Beginner","🛠️","orange","Use browser tools to inspect and debug webpages.","DevTools commonly provides Elements, Console, Network, Sources, Application, and Performance panels. Learn to inspect the exact failing request or element instead of guessing.","console.log('debug');","Open DevTools and inspect a broken button's HTML and event-related errors.","Which panel commonly shows JavaScript errors?","Console"],["Network Debugging","Debugging","Intermediate","📡","orange","Diagnose failed API and asset requests in the Network panel.","The Network panel shows request URLs, methods, status codes, timing, headers, and response data. It is useful for 404, CORS, authentication, and slow-request problems.","fetch('/api/stats') // inspect this request in Network","Find one API request and record its method, status, and response size.","Where can you inspect an HTTP request's status and response?","The Network panel."],["JavaScript Console Techniques","Debugging","Beginner","🧪","orange","Use logs and assertions to inspect program state.","console.log helps inspect values. console.table displays arrays and objects as tables. console.assert reports unexpected conditions.","console.table([{topic:'HTML',score:90},{topic:'CSS',score:85}]);","Use console.table to inspect a list of five lessons.","Which console method displays tabular data?","console.table()"],["Algorithm Big O Basics","Algorithms","Intermediate","🧠","orange","Estimate how work grows as input size increases.","Big O describes an algorithm's growth rate. O(1) is constant, O(n) grows linearly, and O(n²) often comes from nested loops over the same collection.","for(const item of items){console.log(item)} // O(n)","Classify three simple loops as O(1), O(n), or O(n²).","What does O(n) generally describe?","Work that grows linearly with input size."],["Searching Arrays","Algorithms","Beginner","🔎","orange","Compare linear search with faster indexed approaches.","Linear search checks items one by one and is O(n). Binary search can be O(log n) when data is sorted and random access is available.","const found=items.find(x=>x.id===42);","Search an array of lesson objects by ID.","What is the typical time complexity of linear search?","O(n)"],["Sorting Concepts","Algorithms","Intermediate","↕️","orange","Understand why sorting is useful and how comparison functions work.","Sorting arranges values according to a rule. In JavaScript, provide a comparator for numeric sorting because default sort compares string representations.","[10,2,30].sort((a,b)=>a-b);","Sort lesson scores from highest to lowest.","Why provide a comparator for numeric JavaScript sorting?","Default sort compares values as strings."],["Stacks and Queues","Algorithms","Intermediate","📚","orange","Model last-in-first-out and first-in-first-out behavior.","A stack is LIFO: the last item added is removed first. A queue is FIFO: the first item added is removed first. Both appear in scheduling and traversal algorithms.","const stack=[];stack.push('A');stack.pop();","Model browser history as a stack of pages.","Which structure is FIFO?","Queue"],["Recursion Basics","Algorithms","Advanced","🌀","orange","Solve some problems by reducing them to smaller versions.","A recursive function calls itself and needs a base case. Without a base case recursion can continue indefinitely.","function count(n){if(n===0)return;console.log(n);count(n-1)}","Write a recursive countdown with a clear base case.","What prevents recursion from continuing forever?","A base case."],["Cloud Deployment Concepts","Cloud","Beginner","☁️","blue","Understand hosting, builds, domains, and environment configuration.","Deployment moves an application from source code to an environment users can access. Modern platforms often build from Git, serve static assets, and connect to APIs.","Git push → build → deploy → live URL","Draw the deployment flow for your StudyHub frontend.","What is deployment?","Making a software version available in a target environment."],["Environment Variables","Cloud","Intermediate","🔐","blue","Separate configuration from source code where appropriate.","Environment variables can hold configuration that changes between environments. Secrets should be stored using the platform's secret-management system, not committed to source control.","API_URL=https://example.com","List three values that might differ between development and production.","Why separate configuration from code?","To make deployments safer and easier to configure."],["CDN Basics","Cloud","Intermediate","🌍","blue","Understand how content delivery networks serve assets closer to users.","A CDN caches and serves static resources from distributed edge locations. It can reduce latency and origin load when configured well.","Browser → CDN edge → origin","Identify which StudyHub assets could be cached at the edge.","What is a major purpose of a CDN?","Serve content efficiently from locations closer to users."],["Caching Headers","Cloud","Advanced","⚡","blue","Control how browsers and intermediaries reuse responses.","Cache-Control headers describe caching behavior. Long-lived immutable assets can be cached aggressively, while dynamic responses may need no-store or revalidation.","Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable","Choose caching behavior for a versioned CSS file and a live stats endpoint.","Which header controls caching behavior?","Cache-Control"],["Database Normalization","Databases","Advanced","🗂️","purple","Reduce unnecessary duplication by organizing relational data.","Normalization separates related concepts into tables and relationships. It can reduce update anomalies, though real systems sometimes use deliberate denormalization for performance.","students(id,name)\ncourses(id,title)\nenrollments(student_id,course_id)","Split a duplicated student-course dataset into related tables.","What problem can normalization reduce?","Unnecessary duplication and update anomalies."],["Database Pagination","Databases","Intermediate","📄","purple","Return large datasets in manageable pages.","Pagination limits how many rows an API or database query returns at once. Offset pagination is simple; cursor-based pagination can be better for changing large datasets.","SELECT * FROM lessons LIMIT 20 OFFSET 40;","Design an API that returns 20 lessons per page.","Why paginate large results?","To reduce response size and processing work."],["Database Constraints","Databases","Intermediate","🛡️","purple","Let the database enforce important data rules.","Constraints such as NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, PRIMARY KEY, and FOREIGN KEY prevent invalid states. Application validation should complement, not replace, database rules.","score INTEGER CHECK(score BETWEEN 0 AND 100)","Add three constraints to a student table.","Why use database constraints?","To enforce data integrity at the database level."],["AI Prompt Basics","AI","Beginner","🤖","pink","Write clear instructions that give an AI enough context to help.","Useful prompts state the goal, relevant context, constraints, and desired output. Specific examples can reduce ambiguity.","Goal: explain loops\nLevel: beginner\nFormat: short example + practice","Rewrite a vague question into a goal, context, constraints, and output format.","What makes a prompt easier to follow?","A clear goal, relevant context, constraints, and expected output."],["AI Verification","AI","Beginner","🔎","pink","Check generated answers instead of assuming they are correct.","AI output can contain mistakes. Verify important facts against reliable sources, run generated code, inspect calculations, and compare claims with documentation.","Generate → inspect → test → verify","Take one generated code example and test its assumptions before using it.","Why verify AI-generated information?","Generated output can contain errors or unsupported claims."],["AI Coding Workflow","AI","Intermediate","🧑‍💻","pink","Use AI as a coding assistant while keeping control of the project.","A productive workflow is explain the task, provide relevant code, request a small change, review the diff, test it, and iterate. Avoid accepting large changes blindly.","Task → Context → Small change → Review → Test","Ask an AI assistant to improve one function and then inspect the resulting diff.","What should happen before accepting a generated code change?","Review and test it."],["AI Study Assistant","AI","Beginner","📚","pink","Use AI to generate practice questions and explanations responsibly.","AI can help turn notes into quizzes, flashcards, examples, and alternative explanations. Treat generated material as a draft and verify important details.","Notes → Questions → Answer → Check → Review","Turn one lesson into five recall questions and answer them yourself first.","What is a useful study use of AI?","Generating practice material and alternative explanations."],["Pomodoro Study Method","Study Skills","Beginner","🍅","pink","Use focused work intervals and deliberate breaks.","A Pomodoro-style session uses a focused interval followed by a short break. The exact timing can be adjusted to your concentration and task.","25 min focus → 5 min break → repeat","Run two focused study intervals and record what you completed.","What is the main purpose of a study break?","Recover attention before the next focused session."],["Goal Setting","Study Skills","Beginner","🎯","pink","Turn broad ambitions into specific learning targets.","A useful learning goal states what you will be able to do, not only what you will read. Break large goals into observable milestones.","Goal: Build a responsive card page by Sunday.","Write a one-week goal with three measurable milestones.","What makes a learning goal useful?","It describes an observable outcome and manageable milestones."],["Learning Journal","Study Skills","Beginner","📓","pink","Track what you learned, what confused you, and what to review.","A short learning journal can capture key ideas, mistakes, questions, and next steps. Reviewing it creates a feedback loop.","Learned: flexbox alignment\nConfused: flex-basis\nNext: build a card grid","Write a five-minute journal entry after today's lesson.","What should a learning journal capture?","Key learning, questions, mistakes, and next steps."],["Practice Projects","Study Skills","Intermediate","🛠️","pink","Convert concepts into working projects to deepen understanding.","Projects force you to combine ideas, make decisions, debug errors, and remember concepts through use. Start small and finish before expanding scope.","Learn → Build tiny → Test → Improve","Choose one topic and build a project that uses three concepts from it.","Why are small projects useful?","They turn abstract knowledge into practical experience."],["Exam Revision Plan","Study Skills","Intermediate","🗓️","pink","Organize revision around topics, recall, practice, and weak areas.","Start by listing topics, assess confidence, schedule recall sessions, and leave time for practice questions. Spend extra time where errors repeat.","Topics → Confidence → Schedule → Recall → Practice","Create a seven-day revision plan for three subjects.","What should receive extra review time?","Topics where practice reveals repeated errors or weak recall."],["Focus and Distraction Control","Study Skills","Beginner","🎧","pink","Create an environment that makes focused study easier.","Reduce notifications, keep only needed tabs open, define one task, and use a visible timer or checklist. Environmental changes can reduce unnecessary context switching.","One task + phone away + timer + checklist","Do a 20-minute distraction audit and remove three common interruptions.","What is context switching?","Frequently moving attention between different tasks."],["Note Taking Systems","Study Skills","Intermediate","🗒️","pink","Capture ideas in a form that supports later recall.","Good notes emphasize concepts, examples, questions, and connections rather than copying everything. Cornell-style, outline, and concept-map methods are possible approaches.","Concept → Example → Question → Summary","Convert one page of notes into five questions and a short summary.","What should notes support after class?","Understanding, recall, review, and connections."],["Learning from Errors","Study Skills","Advanced","🔁","pink","Turn mistakes into targeted practice.","When you miss a question, identify whether the problem was knowledge, interpretation, procedure, or carelessness. Then create a small corrective exercise.","Mistake → Cause → Correction → New attempt","Keep an error log for five practice questions.","Why classify mistakes?","It helps choose a targeted correction instead of repeating the same practice."],["Capstone: Portfolio Project","Project","Grandmaster","🏆","green","Combine your skills into a polished project you can demonstrate.","A portfolio project should solve a real problem, have a clear interface, document its setup, and explain important technical choices. Finish a small version before adding advanced features.","Idea → Scope → Build → Test → Deploy → Document","Create a project brief with goal, users, features, stack, and milestones.","What should a portfolio project demonstrate?","A useful outcome, working implementation, clear choices, and documentation."],["HTML Forms","HTML","Beginner","📝","blue","Build forms with labels, inputs, buttons, and useful field types.","Forms collect user input. Pair labels with controls, choose meaningful input types, and provide a clear submit action.","<label for=\"email\">Email</label><input id=\"email\" type=\"email\">","Build a signup form with name, email, password, and a submit button.","Why should inputs have labels?","Labels make controls clearer and improve accessibility."],["HTML Form Validation","HTML","Intermediate","✅","blue","Create helpful client-side validation without replacing server validation.","required, type, pattern, min, max, and length attributes can provide immediate feedback. Server-side validation is still required for trusted data.","<input type=\"email\" required>","Create a form that validates an email and minimum password length.","Can client-side validation replace server validation?","No."],["HTML SVG Basics","HTML","Intermediate","✏️","blue","Create scalable vector graphics directly in markup.","SVG is useful for icons, diagrams, and simple illustrations because vectors scale without becoming pixelated.","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"40\"/></svg>","Draw a simple circle and rectangle with SVG.","Why are SVG graphics useful for icons?","They scale cleanly at different sizes."],["HTML Web Storage","HTML","Intermediate","💾","blue","Store small browser-side values with localStorage and sessionStorage.","localStorage persists across browser sessions while sessionStorage is tied to a tab session. Do not store sensitive secrets in either.","localStorage.setItem('theme','bright');","Save a user's theme preference and restore it on page load.","Which storage normally persists after closing the browser?","localStorage"],["CSS Flexbox","CSS","Beginner","↔️","purple","Align and distribute items along one dimension.","Flexbox is ideal for rows or columns of related items. Learn display:flex, gap, justify-content, align-items, and flex-wrap.","display:flex;gap:16px;justify-content:space-between;","Create a responsive navigation row that wraps on small screens.","Which property distributes items along the main axis?","justify-content"],["CSS Grid","CSS","Beginner","▦","purple","Build two-dimensional layouts with rows and columns.","Grid provides explicit rows and columns and is useful for cards, dashboards, and page layouts.","display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;","Create a three-column lesson card grid.","Which property defines grid columns?","grid-template-columns"],["CSS Media Queries","CSS","Beginner","📱","purple","Apply styles based on viewport or device conditions.","Media queries let a design adapt at breakpoints. Keep breakpoints based on content needs rather than specific devices.","@media (max-width:700px){.cards{grid-template-columns:1fr;}}","Make a desktop grid become one column on narrow screens.","Which at-rule creates responsive conditions?","@media"],["CSS Pseudo Classes","CSS","Beginner","✨","purple","Style interactive states such as hover, focus, and checked.","Pseudo-classes represent element states. Use :hover for pointer interaction and :focus-visible for keyboard focus.","button:hover{transform:translateY(-2px)}","Style a button differently when focused with the keyboard.","Which pseudo-class targets keyboard-visible focus?","focus-visible"],["CSS Transforms","CSS","Beginner","🔄","purple","Move, scale, rotate, and skew elements without changing normal flow.","Transforms are useful for subtle interaction and visual effects. Pair them with transitions when appropriate.",".card:hover{transform:translateY(-4px) scale(1.01)}","Create a subtle hover lift for a lesson card.","Which property applies a transform?","transform"],["CSS Aspect Ratio","CSS","Intermediate","📐","purple","Maintain predictable media proportions.","aspect-ratio lets a box preserve a width-to-height relationship, useful for video thumbnails and image cards.",".thumb{aspect-ratio:16/9;overflow:hidden}","Create a 16:9 lesson thumbnail component.","Which property preserves a chosen ratio?","aspect-ratio"],["JavaScript DOM Selection","JavaScript","Beginner","🎯","orange","Find elements in a webpage before changing them.","querySelector returns the first matching element and querySelectorAll returns all matches. Use clear selectors and avoid unnecessary repeated queries.","const title=document.querySelector('h1');title.textContent='StudyHub';","Select a lesson title and change its text.","Which method selects the first matching CSS selector?","querySelector()"],["JavaScript DOM Events","JavaScript","Beginner","🖱️","orange","Respond to clicks, input, submit, and other browser events.","addEventListener attaches a function to an event. Use event objects to inspect what happened and prevent default behavior when needed.","button.addEventListener('click',()=>alert('Started'));","Add a click handler to a Start Lesson button.","Which method attaches an event listener?","addEventListener()"],["JavaScript DOM Creation","JavaScript","Intermediate","🏗️","orange","Create and insert elements dynamically.","createElement creates a node. append and appendChild can insert it. Keep generated content escaped when it contains user-provided text.","const li=document.createElement('li');li.textContent='Python';list.append(li);","Generate five lesson list items from an array.","Which method creates a new HTML element node?","document.createElement()"],["JavaScript LocalStorage","JavaScript","Beginner","💾","orange","Persist small non-sensitive preferences in the browser.","localStorage stores strings, so JSON.stringify and JSON.parse are useful for arrays and objects. Never treat localStorage as secure storage.","localStorage.setItem('progress',JSON.stringify([1,2]));","Save and restore a small array of completed lesson IDs.","What type of values does localStorage directly store?","Strings"],["JavaScript Promise.all","JavaScript","Advanced","⚡","orange","Run independent asynchronous tasks together and await all results.","Promise.all resolves when every promise resolves and rejects if one rejects. It is useful for independent requests that can run concurrently.","const [stats,lessons]=await Promise.all([getStats(),getLessons()]);","Load two independent API resources concurrently.","When does Promise.all reject?","When one of its promises rejects."],["JavaScript Closures","JavaScript","Advanced","🔒","orange","Understand functions that retain access to surrounding variables.","A closure occurs when a function keeps access to variables from its lexical scope after the outer function has returned.","function counter(){let n=0;return()=>++n}","Create a counter function that remembers its count.","What does a closure preserve access to?","Variables from its lexical scope."],["Python Decorators","Python","Advanced","🎁","green","Wrap functions to add reusable behavior.","A decorator receives a function and returns a wrapped function. Decorators are common for logging, authorization, caching, and framework features.","def log_call(fn):\n    def wrapper(*args,**kwargs):\n        print('calling');return fn(*args,**kwargs)\n    return wrapper","Create a decorator that logs when a function starts.","What does a decorator typically do?","Wrap a function to add behavior."],["Python Generators","Python","Advanced","⚙️","green","Produce values lazily with yield.","Generators calculate values as needed instead of storing the entire sequence in memory. They are useful for large streams of data.","def count_up(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        yield i","Create a generator that yields lesson IDs one at a time.","Which keyword produces a value from a generator?","yield"],["Python SQLite","Python","Intermediate","🗄️","green","Use SQLite for lightweight local relational storage.","Python's sqlite3 module provides a built-in interface to SQLite. Use parameterized queries rather than string concatenation for values.","import sqlite3\ncon=sqlite3.connect('study.db')","Create a tiny database with a lessons table and insert one row.","Which Python module provides SQLite support?","sqlite3"],["SQL Subqueries","SQL","Intermediate","🔍","purple","Use one query inside another when it clarifies a data problem.","A subquery can produce a value or set of rows used by an outer query.","SELECT name FROM students WHERE score>(SELECT AVG(score) FROM students);","Find students whose score is above the overall average.","What is a subquery?","A query nested inside another query."],["SQL CTEs","SQL","Advanced","🧩","purple","Break complex SQL into named query steps with WITH.","CTEs improve readability and can simplify multi-stage queries.","WITH top AS (SELECT * FROM students WHERE score>=90) SELECT * FROM top;","Rewrite a nested query using a CTE.","Which keyword begins a CTE?","WITH"],["SQL Window Functions","SQL","Expert","🪟","purple","Calculate rankings and running values without collapsing rows.","Window functions calculate across related rows while keeping individual rows visible.","SELECT name,score,RANK() OVER(ORDER BY score DESC) FROM students;","Rank students while keeping every row.","What does a window function preserve?","Individual result rows."],["HTTP Headers","Web","Beginner","📨","blue","Understand metadata sent with HTTP requests and responses.","Headers describe content, caching, authorization, language preferences, and other request or response properties.","Content-Type: application/json","Identify three useful API headers.","What does Content-Type describe?","The media type of the message body."],["HTTP Authentication","Web","Intermediate","🔑","blue","Understand how APIs identify clients and control access.","Authentication establishes identity while authorization controls allowed actions. APIs may use sessions, bearer tokens, API keys, or other schemes.","Authorization: Bearer TOKEN","Explain authentication versus authorization.","What is authorization?","Determining what an authenticated identity may do."],["Web HTTPS","Web","Beginner","🔒","blue","Understand encrypted browser-to-server communication.","HTTPS uses TLS to protect data in transit and authenticate the server through certificates.","https://example.com","Explain what HTTPS protects while data travels.","What protects HTTPS traffic?","TLS"],["Web DNS Basics","Web","Beginner","🌐","blue","Understand how domain names map to network services.","DNS maps names to records such as A, AAAA, CNAME, and MX.","example.com → DNS lookup → address","Identify the record commonly used for IPv4 addresses.","Which record maps a name to an IPv4 address?","A"],["Security Password Hashing","Security","Beginner","🔐","pink","Understand why passwords should use dedicated password-hashing algorithms.","Password storage should use algorithms such as Argon2, bcrypt, or scrypt with appropriate parameters rather than plaintext or ordinary fast hashes.","password → password hash → stored hash","Explain why plaintext passwords are unsafe.","Why use password hashing?","To make recovered password databases harder to use for guessing."],["Security XSS Concepts","Security","Intermediate","🛡️","pink","Understand cross-site scripting and safer output handling.","XSS occurs when untrusted content becomes executable markup or script in a browser. Use contextual output encoding and safe DOM APIs such as textContent.","element.textContent=userInput;","Find unsafe HTML insertion and suggest a safer approach.","What is a safer alternative to innerHTML for plain text?","textContent"],["Security SQL Injection","Security","Intermediate","💉","pink","Understand how untrusted input can alter dynamically built SQL.","SQL injection occurs when application input is combined into SQL syntax unsafely. Parameterized queries separate values from SQL.","SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?","Rewrite a string-concatenated query using parameters.","What is the primary defense against SQL injection?","Parameterized queries."],["Algorithms Binary Search","Algorithms","Intermediate","🔍","orange","Search sorted data efficiently by repeatedly halving the search space.","Binary search requires sorted data and usually runs in O(log n) time.","left=0; right=items.length-1;","Implement binary search for sorted lesson IDs.","What prerequisite does binary search require?","Sorted data."],["Algorithms Hash Tables","Algorithms","Intermediate","🗂️","orange","Use key-value structures for fast average lookup.","Hash tables map keys to values. JavaScript Map and Python dictionaries are common examples.","const scores=new Map([['AJ',95]]);","Build a Map from usernames to XP.","What is a key advantage of hash-table lookup?","Fast average-case lookup by key."],["Algorithms Trees","Algorithms","Advanced","🌳","orange","Represent hierarchical data with nodes and relationships.","Trees model file systems, DOM structures, categories, and many search structures.","root → subjects → lessons","Draw a tree representing StudyHub subjects.","What is the top node called?","Root"],["Cloud Docker Basics","Cloud","Beginner","🐳","blue","Package an application and its runtime into a container image.","Docker images describe an application environment and containers provide isolated processes.","FROM python:3.13\nCOPY app.py /app.py","Write a tiny Dockerfile for a Python app.","What is a Dockerfile used for?","Defining how to build a container image."],["Cloud CI Basics","Cloud","Intermediate","🔄","blue","Automate tests and checks when code changes.","Continuous integration runs automated checks for new commits or pull requests.","push → install → test → build","Design a CI workflow for StudyHub.","What is a common purpose of CI?","Automatically verify changes before integration."],["AI Tokens and Context","AI","Beginner","🧩","pink","Understand tokenized input and finite model context.","Language models process text in tokens. Context limits constrain how much information can be considered in one request.","prompt → tokens → model → output","Explain why long documents may need chunking.","Why can long inputs require chunking?","Models have finite context capacity."],["AI Embeddings","AI","Intermediate","🧭","pink","Represent text as vectors for similarity-based retrieval.","Embeddings map text into numeric vectors. Similarity measures can help retrieve related documents.","query → embedding → similarity search","Design semantic search for StudyHub lessons.","What are embeddings useful for?","Representing content for similarity-based retrieval."],["AI RAG Basics","AI","Intermediate","📚","pink","Combine retrieval of relevant documents with model generation.","RAG retrieves relevant source material and supplies it to a model so answers can be grounded in that context.","question → retrieve → context → answer","Design a StudyHub assistant grounded only in its lesson library.","What does RAG add before generation?","Retrieved relevant context."],["AI Evaluation","AI","Advanced","🧪","pink","Measure an AI feature using representative tests.","Evaluation sets should reflect real tasks, edge cases, and failure modes.","test set → run → score → inspect","Create ten evaluation questions for a StudyHub tutor.","Why use an evaluation set?","To measure behavior consistently."],["Study Feynman Technique","Study Skills","Intermediate","🗣️","pink","Test understanding by explaining a concept simply.","Explain an idea without jargon, identify gaps, relearn, and explain again.","learn → explain → find gaps → relearn","Explain CSS Grid to a beginner in five sentences.","What does this technique reveal?","Gaps in understanding."],["Study Interleaving","Study Skills","Advanced","🔀","pink","Mix related problem types instead of practicing only one type.","Interleaving requires you to identify which method fits each problem.","HTML → CSS → JS → HTML → CSS","Create mixed practice from three subjects.","What skill does interleaving strengthen?","Choosing the right method."],["Project Quiz App","Project","Intermediate","❓","green","Build a quiz interface with questions, scoring, and progress.","A quiz app combines data modeling, events, state, rendering, and persistence.","questions → render → answer → score","Build a five-question quiz with a score screen.","What state must a quiz track?","Current question, answers or score, and completion state."],["Project REST API","Project","Advanced","🔌","green","Build a small API with resources, validation, errors, and persistence.","A REST-style project combines routing, HTTP methods, JSON, validation, database access, and consistent errors.","GET /lessons\nPOST /progress","Plan a lesson-progress API with four endpoints.","What should a good API define consistently?","Resources, methods, validation, response shapes, and errors."],["Project Pomodoro Timer","Project","Intermediate","⏲️","green","Build a study timer with start, pause, reset, and completion feedback.","Timers combine state, intervals, button events, formatting, and cleanup.","10:00 → Start → 09:59 → Pause → Reset","Build a ten-minute practice timer.","Why clear an interval when a timer stops?","To prevent duplicate updates and wasted work."]].map(([title,subject,level,icon,color,description,learn,example,practice,quiz,answer]) => ({title,subject,level,icon,color,description,content:{learn,example,practice,quiz,answer}}));
